The '''Araucarian Passage''' ({{Langx|bl|Pasaje de Araucano}}), also known as the '''Araucarian Crescent''' or the '''Araucarian Way''' was a network of secret routes and [[W:Safe house|safe houses]] established in the [[Balisca|Baliscan archipelago]] beginning into the late 18th-century, and used by [[W:History of slavery|enslaved]] people to escape into the [[Maroon Confederacy|Maroon Territory]], which was essentially ''{{W|terra incognita}}'' due to the [[Maroon Wars]]. The scheme prior to Baliscan independence was assisted by [[Catharism|Cathar communities]] and others sympathetic to the cause of the [[W:Maroons|escapees]].

At the 1808 [[Granyana Convention]], the insurgent [[Southern Confederation]] issued the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]], in which it pledged to "fully eradicate" the institution of slavery and guaranteed freedom and protection to all formerly enslaved persons. From this point, the confederation emerged as the prominent destination for runaway slaves ("[[W:Maroons|maroons]]") seeking freedom, as its porous maritime borders gave many points of access. Most former enslaved reached Baliscan territory by crossing the [[W:Ilhas Cagarras|Cagarras Strait]] from [[W:Rio de Janeiro (state)|Rio de Janeiro]] and other Brazilian ports into [[Pindorama]]. While some opted to settle in Pindorama, the majority continued their journey across [[Atlántida]] towards the Maroon Territory. Due to the alignment of much of Bayara with the Spanish royalists, most refugees opted to take the much longer route through [[Hesperia]] to avoid [[W:Slave patrol|slave patrols]]. Smaller numbers escaped the European colonies by crossing the {{W|Argentine Sea}} from {{W|Patagonia}}, through the [[Malvinas]] and into [[Cerdanya]]. 

The practice eventually led to a severe [[W:Diplomatic incident|diplomatic incident]] between the Southern Confederation and the [[W:Empire of Brazil|Empire of Brazil]], with the Brazilians demanding an immediate halt to the ferrying, and the reparation of all of the runaway slaves that had been resettled in Balisca territory. The republics refused reparation of individuals whom it considered under its protection, but splintered over the question of intercepting fugitive boats and sending them back to Brazil, with Bayaran delegates strongly in favor of repatriation. Bayaran ships were ordered to begin searching and seizing any vessels that were found to be harboring runaways, greatly reducing the number of fugitives. These Bayaran policies would inflame the [[Confederal Question]], and contributed to the eventual [[Basilio mutiny]] and the [[Baliscan Civil War]]. 

Following the 1838 accession to the [[Afro Atlantic Charter]], the [[Anyos Federales|federal Baliscan state]] began providing significant aid to these runaway slaves, utilizing hundreds of fishing boats to [[W:Island hopping|ferry runaways]] from ports in Brazil, and then smuggling them across the Baliscan islands to [[W:Quilombo|quilombos]] in Araucaria. More than # people were said to have escaped there via the network during its 20-year peak period.

